WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.047 --> 00:00:03.047
(electronic music)

00:00:04.490 --> 00:00:06.360
- [Reporter] An average
of 30 container ships

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:08.950
has sat anchored daily just off the coast

00:00:08.950 --> 00:00:10.660
of Los Angeles this year.

00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:13.550
- The normal number of container ships

00:00:13.550 --> 00:00:16.420
at anchor is zero to one.

00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:18.970
- [Reporter] This ship, the
Ever Front, not to be confused

00:00:18.970 --> 00:00:23.120
with the now-famous Ever
Given was waiting for 13 days.

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:24.490
- Seeing vessels at anchorage

00:00:24.490 --> 00:00:27.250
outside the nation's
largest container port

00:00:27.250 --> 00:00:29.000
does not happen every day

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:33.610
and it is a once in about a
decade situation that we see.

00:00:33.610 --> 00:00:34.560
- [Reporter] Ever Front was part

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:36.350
of a bottleneck in the supply chain

00:00:36.350 --> 00:00:38.590
that's still straining
the neighboring ports

00:00:38.590 --> 00:00:41.230
which handle about a
third of all U.S. imports.

00:00:41.230 --> 00:00:42.820
- I don't think it's
an exaggeration to say

00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:45.750
that the ports have never
really seen this confluence

00:00:45.750 --> 00:00:47.940
of stresses that they've had to deal with.

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:50.490
- [Reporter] So what's
causing this off shore queue?

00:00:50.490 --> 00:00:53.360
How do these waiting ships
impact customers onshore?

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:55.163
And when will it return to normal?

00:00:56.130 --> 00:00:59.143
- I think we got here because
we all had to stay home.

00:01:00.220 --> 00:01:01.680
- [Reporter] Experts
say if there's one cause

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:04.610
for the backup, it's COVID-19.

00:01:04.610 --> 00:01:06.180
- Staying home leads you to go

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:09.510
all right, my desk isn't
great if I've got one.

00:01:09.510 --> 00:01:12.230
My chair isn't comfortable,
even if I've got one.

00:01:12.230 --> 00:01:14.410
I might need a new computer monitor.

00:01:14.410 --> 00:01:17.410
I might need a webcam.

00:01:17.410 --> 00:01:18.910
Actually, I can't go to the gym,

00:01:18.910 --> 00:01:20.730
so I'm gonna go buy a Peloton

00:01:20.730 --> 00:01:23.130
or a running machine or some dumbbells.

00:01:23.130 --> 00:01:24.760
- [Reporter] For many
Americans that has meant

00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:27.020
placing online orders for those items,

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:29.540
most of which aren't made in the U.S.

00:01:29.540 --> 00:01:32.120
Seaborne imports to the
U.S. started surging

00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:34.570
in the late summer as
manufacturers and retailers

00:01:34.570 --> 00:01:37.370
tried to rebuild inventories
that were depleted by shoppers

00:01:37.370 --> 00:01:39.680
in the early months of the pandemic.

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:41.870
- We had 57 extra ships arrive

00:01:41.870 --> 00:01:44.660
in the fall above normal levels

00:01:44.660 --> 00:01:47.990
which choked the system
because at that same time

00:01:47.990 --> 00:01:50.980
the goods movement system
ashore, the terminals,

00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:53.010
the trains, the trucks, the warehouses,

00:01:53.010 --> 00:01:55.360
the forklift drivers had some fraction

00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:58.030
of the workforce out with COVID

00:01:58.030 --> 00:02:01.770
and a different fraction out
because of contact tracing.

00:02:01.770 --> 00:02:03.730
So just when there's more cargo,

00:02:03.730 --> 00:02:05.510
there's a less efficient goods movement

00:02:05.510 --> 00:02:08.223
in the system ashore,
so the ships backed up.

00:02:09.570 --> 00:02:11.310
- [Reporter] But shipments
continued, peaking

00:02:11.310 --> 00:02:14.480
in February when 177
container ships carrying

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:17.020
more than 800,000 containers arrived

00:02:17.020 --> 00:02:18.910
at the Southern California ports.

00:02:18.910 --> 00:02:22.790
That's 31% more ships
and 49% more containers

00:02:22.790 --> 00:02:24.290
than the same month last year.

00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:27.560
This gap highlights a related issue.

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:30.210
The ships are getting bigger.

00:02:30.210 --> 00:02:32.330
- There's been a significant
increase in the number

00:02:32.330 --> 00:02:34.920
of the very biggest boats that come in.

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:37.060
Whilst they're very efficient
when they're at sea,

00:02:37.060 --> 00:02:40.240
they're a devil to unpack
and a devil to park

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:44.170
and so it takes longer to
get all of them sorted out.

00:02:44.170 --> 00:02:46.700
- [Reporter] So the ships
continue to wait an average

00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:49.390
of seven and a half days
before reaching the port.

00:02:49.390 --> 00:02:52.023
And that means consumers are waiting too.

00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:54.613
- Okay. Peloton, let's go.

00:02:56.550 --> 00:02:57.600
- [Reporter] This is something buyers

00:02:57.600 --> 00:02:59.820
of Peloton bikes may already know.

00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:01.770
The company said in its
latest earnings report

00:03:01.770 --> 00:03:04.690
that backups contributed
to recent shipping delays.

00:03:04.690 --> 00:03:07.280
- I'm sure you've seen that
on a lot of e-commerce sites

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:10.170
it's taking things longer
than normal to deliver to you.

00:03:10.170 --> 00:03:11.400
And so, you know, there's

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:13.600
that kind of customer disappointment angle

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:14.860
and companies will then need to think

00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:16.860
okay, how do I avoid that in the future.

00:03:18.010 --> 00:03:21.040
- Peloton, come on, let's
get this workout started.

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:23.950
- [Reporter] For Peloton, the
answer has been to spend more.

00:03:23.950 --> 00:03:25.990
The company said it would
spend a hundred million dollars

00:03:25.990 --> 00:03:28.100
in transportation,
including air shipments,

00:03:28.100 --> 00:03:29.840
in the first half of
the year to get around

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:31.960
the bottlenecks at the port.

00:03:31.960 --> 00:03:34.170
Mom-and-pop retailers also have products

00:03:34.170 --> 00:03:36.940
they'd hoped to sell stuck on these ships.

00:03:36.940 --> 00:03:38.880
Many of the smallest shops can't afford

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:41.110
to fly products in instead,

00:03:41.110 --> 00:03:43.550
but the ships aren't the only issue here.

00:03:43.550 --> 00:03:46.500
The entire supply chain,
from ports to warehouses

00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:50.060
to distribution centers,
is inundated with cargo.

00:03:50.060 --> 00:03:53.600
- So if the cargo is not
moving quickly from the ship

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:56.880
to the shore, out through the
warehouses, to the consumer,

00:03:56.880 --> 00:03:59.720
everything else is gonna start to back up.

00:03:59.720 --> 00:04:01.210
- [Reporter] This
congestion, which was driven

00:04:01.210 --> 00:04:03.220
by demand for shipping, also means

00:04:03.220 --> 00:04:05.970
that the cost of shipping has gone up.

00:04:05.970 --> 00:04:08.080
The current global average
shipping rate is nearly

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:10.830
four times what it was in January, 2020,

00:04:10.830 --> 00:04:13.450
according to data from
S &amp; P Global Platts.

00:04:13.450 --> 00:04:14.630
- Someone's gonna pay for that.

00:04:14.630 --> 00:04:16.590
It's either gonna come
off company's profits

00:04:16.590 --> 00:04:18.940
or they're gonna pass
it through to consumers.

00:04:18.940 --> 00:04:21.000
The longer it lasts, the more likely it is

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:22.600
that consumers are gonna pay it.

00:04:24.093 --> 00:04:25.560
(tones chiming)

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:27.180
- [Reporter] At the
ports, workers are pushing

00:04:27.180 --> 00:04:29.600
to get the anchored ships moving.

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:31.880
- Pre-pandemic, we were welcoming

00:04:31.880 --> 00:04:36.280
on average 10 container
vessels in to work at the port

00:04:36.280 --> 00:04:39.050
loading and unloading
containers every day.

00:04:39.050 --> 00:04:43.220
During this surge we've
been averaging 15 vessels

00:04:43.220 --> 00:04:44.920
working every day in the port.

00:04:44.920 --> 00:04:47.230
That's a 50% jump in productivity

00:04:47.230 --> 00:04:50.020
alongside our vessels every day.

00:04:50.020 --> 00:04:50.853
- [Reporter] Seroka says

00:04:50.853 --> 00:04:52.660
the port's short-term strategy focuses

00:04:52.660 --> 00:04:55.980
on vaccinating workers,
maximizing efficiencies,

00:04:55.980 --> 00:04:59.390
and asking importers to
more quickly pick up cargo.

00:04:59.390 --> 00:05:01.970
That, he predicts, will
help clear the backlog

00:05:01.970 --> 00:05:03.410
of ships by this summer.

00:05:03.410 --> 00:05:06.200
Any longer could bring more problems.

00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:07.770
- We have a short window here

00:05:07.770 --> 00:05:10.630
before our traditional
peak season kicks off

00:05:10.630 --> 00:05:14.180
in August for back to school,
other retail requirements,

00:05:14.180 --> 00:05:16.960
and the all-important year-end holiday.

00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:18.510
- [Reporter] With Americans
receiving a fresh round

00:05:18.510 --> 00:05:21.430
of stimulus, this
bottleneck could intensify

00:05:21.430 --> 00:05:23.530
and how states and the
federal government proceed

00:05:23.530 --> 00:05:26.020
with reopening may impact that.

00:05:26.020 --> 00:05:28.100
- If we're gonna spend
another summer locked down,

00:05:28.100 --> 00:05:29.640
then people are gonna be buying, you know,

00:05:29.640 --> 00:05:31.410
new lawn chairs and a whole bunch of stuff

00:05:31.410 --> 00:05:33.540
they didn't buy last year.

00:05:33.540 --> 00:05:36.010
If everything's open again
and people can go on vacation,

00:05:36.010 --> 00:05:39.110
it's all services spending,
we're not gonna buy stuff.

00:05:39.110 --> 00:05:42.401
So the demand for shipping will
drop off relatively quickly.

00:05:42.401 --> 00:05:44.984
(upbeat music)

